10:10:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Tinnitus is the perception of sound when there is no external sound source. The “ringing” is generated by the auditory system itself—often somewhere between the inner ear and the brain. A common pathway is: - **Inner-ear damage or hearing loss:** Tiny sensory hair cells in the cochlea can be damaged by aging, loud noise, certain medications, ear disease, and other causes. - **Reduced or distorted signals to the brain:** When those cells no longer send normal sound information, the brain may try to compensate by increasing the “gain” on auditory signals. - **Spontaneous nerve activity gets interpreted as sound:** Random or abnormal activity in auditory nerves and brain circuits can then be perceived as ringing, buzzing, hissing, or whistling. The exact sound varies because different people have different patterns of hearing-system changes. Tinnitus is often more noticeable in quiet settings because there is less outside sound to mask it. Less commonly, tinnitus can come from earwax, middle-ear problems, jaw issues, blood-flow sounds, or medication effects. A clinician should evaluate new or worsening tinnitus—especially if it is in one ear only, pulses with your heartbeat, follows a head injury, or occurs with sudden hearing loss, dizziness, or neurological symptoms.
